[Node.js](https://nodejs.org/) is a JavaScript runtime for building servers, CLIs, and backend services. Nx scales your Node monorepo with caching, distributed task execution, and affected-only builds.

The Node plugin contains generators and executors to manage Node applications within an Nx workspace.

## Requirements

Below is a reference table that matches the most recent major versions of Nx to the versions of Node.js that they officially support, and are tested against.

The Nx policy is to support the LTS versions (i.e. actively maintained even numbered versions) of Node.js, but we will only remove support for older versions in a major version of Nx to avoid unexpected disruption. We may add support for newer LTS versions in a minor version of Nx as long as it would not break existing projects.

{% aside type="note" title="Other Node.js versions" %}
Other versions of Node.js **may** still work without issue for these versions of Nx. Those include versions which are already EOL, or odd version numbers (e.g. 23), which Node.js actively discourages using in production.
{% /aside %}

{% aside type="note" title="Node 26" %}
Node 26 is on the Current release track and enters LTS in October 2026. Nx tests against it in CI and supports it today.
{% /aside %}

| Nx Version     | Node Version                   |
| -------------- | ------------------------------ |
| 23.x (current) | 26.x, 24.x, ^22.12.0           |
| 22.x           | 26.x, 24.x, ^22.12.0, ^20.19.0 |
| 21.x           | 24.x, ^22.12.0, ^20.19.0       |
| 20.x           | 22.x, 20.x, 18.x               |

We intentionally do not include an `"engines"` field in the `package.json` file for Nx in order to allow for user flexibility, but this table should be considered the official compatibility matrix.

This table will be updated from time to time to reflect the latest versions of Node.js that are supported. If you encounter issues with Nx, please make sure you are using a supported version of Node.js before filing an issue.

## Setting up @nx/node

### Installation

{% aside type="note" title="Keep Nx Package Versions In Sync" %}
Make sure to install the `@nx/node` version that matches the version of `nx` in your repository. If the version numbers get out of sync, you can encounter some difficult to debug errors. You can [fix Nx version mismatches with this recipe](/docs/kb/keep-nx-versions-in-sync).
{% /aside %}

In any Nx workspace, you can install `@nx/node` by running the following command:

```shell
nx add @nx/node
```

This will install the correct version of `@nx/node`.

## Using the @nx/node plugin

### Creating applications

You can add a new application with the following:

```shell
nx g @nx/node:application apps/my-new-app
```

You can run your application with `nx serve my-new-app`, which starts it in watch mode.

The `serve` task comes from the first two lines of `src/main.ts`:

```ts
#!/usr/bin/env node
// @nx-target-name: serve
```

`@nx/js/node` picks up files whose shebang names `node`, and `@nx-target-name` sets the task name. The generator adds that plugin to `nx.json`. The plugin runs scripts in watch mode by default, so `nx serve` runs the file with `node --watch` and restarts it on changes without a build step. Set `watch: false` in the plugin options to turn watch off for every script the registration covers, `serve` included. Workspace libraries the application depends on are built first, through `dependsOn` on the `serve` entry in the project configuration. Node runs the TypeScript source directly, which requires Node.js 22.18 or later. These applications are ES modules, with `"type": "module"` in their `package.json`, so relative imports name the `.ts` file (`./message.ts`). The build rewrites them to `.js`.

In workspaces that use TypeScript path aliases, and for esbuild, Jest, or JavaScript (`--js`) applications, the shebang names [`tsx`](https://tsx.is/) instead, which resolves path aliases and extensionless imports. Applications that use webpack, or workspaces with [inference plugins](/docs/plugin-registry) turned off, get an explicit `serve` target in `project.json` instead.

### Creating libraries

Node libraries are a good way to separate features within your organization. To create a Node library run the following command:

```shell
nx g @nx/node:lib libs/my-new-lib

# If you want the library to be buildable or publishable to npm
nx g @nx/node:lib libs/my-new-lib --buildable
nx g @nx/node:lib libs/my-new-lib \
--publishable \
--importPath=@myorg/my-new-lib
```

## Using node

### Testing projects

You can run unit tests with:

```shell
nx test my-new-app
nx test my-new-lib
```

Replace `my-new-app` with the name or your project. This command works for both applications and libraries.

### Building projects

Node applications can be build with:

```shell
nx build my-new-app
```

And if you generated a library with `--buildable`, then you can build a library as well:

```shell
nx build my-new-lib
```

Applications build with `tsc` in [TypeScript workspaces that use project references](/docs/kb/switch-to-workspaces-project-references), and with [Rolldown](/docs/technologies/build-tools/rolldown/introduction) in workspaces that use TypeScript path aliases. Fastify applications use esbuild there instead. Pass `--bundler` when generating the application to choose `tsc`, `rolldown`, `esbuild`, or `webpack`. The `tsc` bundler needs project references.

The output is in a `dist` folder. To change it, set `outDir` in `tsconfig.app.json` for `tsc`, or `output.dir` in `rolldown.config.ts` for Rolldown.

### Application proxies

Generating Node applications has an option to configure other projects in the workspace to proxy API requests. This can be done by passing the `--frontendProject` with the project name you wish to enable proxy support for.

```shell
nx g @nx/node:application apps/my-new-app \
--frontendProject my-react-app
```

### Waiting for the server to be ready

Tasks that depend on `serve` with `waitFor: "ready"`, such as Playwright and Cypress e2e tasks, start once the server reports that it's listening.
Applications generated with the Express, Fastify, Koa, or NestJS framework report it.
Their `src/main.ts` calls `emitReadySignal` from `@nx/js/ready-signal` once the server listens, and their `serve` entry sets `"readyWhen": { "signal": true }`.

To add this to an existing server, check that the root `package.json` lists `@nx/js`, so the server can import it.
If it doesn't, add `@nx/js` at the same version as `nx`:

```shell
npm install -D @nx/js@<nx-version>
```

Call `emitReadySignal` with the server once it listens:

```ts
const server = app.listen(port, host, async () => {
  // Set when Nx waits for this server to be ready
  if (process.env.NX_READY_SIGNAL_SOCKET) {
    try {
      // Function keeps TypeScript and bundlers from rewriting or bundling the import
      const load = new Function('s', 'return import(s)') as (
        s: string
      ) => Promise<{ emitReadySignal(server: unknown): boolean }>;
      (await load('@nx/js/ready-signal')).emitReadySignal(server);
    } catch {
      // @nx/js isn't installed
    }
  }
});
```

Then set `readyWhen` on the `serve` entry in the project configuration:

```json
"serve": {
  "readyWhen": { "signal": true }
}
```

`emitReadySignal` reads the address from the server and passes it to the waiting tasks as `NX_READY_URL`.
Without `readyWhen`, a task that waits for `serve` starts as soon as `serve` starts.
For why the import is guarded, and for the other checks, see [wait for a continuous task to be ready](/docs/reference/project-configuration#wait-for-a-continuous-task-to-be-ready).

### Debugging

#### VSCode integration

When generating Node applications, Nx automatically creates a VSCode debugging configuration:

- **Automatic setup**: A `.vscode/launch.json` file is created with pre-configured debugging settings.
- **Smart port allocation**: Debug ports are automatically assigned starting from 9229, preventing conflicts between multiple applications.
- **Source map support**: Webpack configurations include `devtoolModuleFilenameTemplate` for accurate source mapping.

To debug your Node application in VSCode:

1. Open your workspace in VSCode.
2. Set breakpoints in your TypeScript source files.
3. Go to the Debug panel `(Ctrl+Shift+D)`.
4. Select "Debug `{your-app-name}` with Nx" from the dropdown.
5. Click the play button or press `F5`.

The debugger will automatically:

- Start your application with `nx serve`.
- Attach to the Node.js debugger on the allocated port.
- Stop on breakpoints in your TypeScript source files, which `nx serve` runs directly.

#### Manual debugging

To open the debugger on a specific port, add Node's `--inspect` flag to the shebang in `src/main.ts`, after the existing `node` or `tsx`:

```ts
#!/usr/bin/env -S node --inspect=9230
```

If the application has an explicit `serve` target in `project.json`, set its `port` option or run `nx serve my-new-app --port <number>` instead.

For additional information on Node.js debugging, see the [Node.js debugging getting started guide](https://nodejs.org/en/docs/guides/debugging-getting-started/#inspector-clients).

